WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.825
Everything ends in the
authorization package.

2
00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:07.946
It has to answer five questions about each
AI system's identity: who owns it, how

3
00:00:07.946 --> 00:00:12.987
it authenticates, what it can reach, who's
watching it, and when its access ends.

4
00:00:12.987 --> 00:00:15.738
Each has an artifact
and controls behind it.

5
00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:17.088
Two points stand out.

6
00:00:17.088 --> 00:00:21.846
First, service identification and
authentication, control I A nine,

7
00:00:21.846 --> 00:00:26.011
describes an AI system calling
a tool with its own identity,

8
00:00:26.011 --> 00:00:29.325
and it's in no NIST baseline
and no FedRAMP class.

9
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:31.100
So the program tailors it in.

10
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.814
Second, three of the seven high-impact
practices can be evidenced by the identity

11
00:00:35.814 --> 00:00:39.543
program: monitoring from the
system's own logs, training checked

12
00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:43.412
against the operators' group, and a
fail-safe that's a tested disable.

13
00:00:43.412 --> 00:00:48.676
The package can be machine-readable in OSCAL,
with no AI-specific structures needed.

14
00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:50.773
NIST's overlays for AI systems,

15
00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:55.138
renamed under the new executive
order, have no public draft yet.

16
00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:58.660
So tailor the baseline now,
and compare when they're final.
